# =============================================================================
# .dockerignore
# =============================================================================
# This file tells Docker's build context which files to EXCLUDE when it
# packages your project directory and sends it to the Docker daemon.
#
# Why does this matter?
#   Before Docker runs a single instruction in your Dockerfile, it tars up
#   the entire build context (the directory you run `docker build` from) and
#   sends it to the Docker daemon.  Without a .dockerignore, that includes
#   node_modules (~500 MB+), dist, .git, screenshots, etc.
#
# Benefits of a .dockerignore:
#   1. SPEED  — The context transfer is the first thing that happens on every
#               build.  Excluding heavy folders makes it near-instant.
#   2. CORRECTNESS — If node_modules is included, it could overwrite the clean
#               `npm ci` install inside the builder stage with your local,
#               potentially OS-specific or stale packages.
#   3. SECURITY — .env files with secrets never enter the build context, so
#               they cannot accidentally leak into an image layer.
#   4. SMALLER IMAGES — Though multi-stage builds already exclude most things,
#               excluding at the context level means they never enter at all.
# =============================================================================


# --- Dependencies (the biggest win) ------------------------------------------
# node_modules can be hundreds of megabytes and is OS-specific (some packages
# compile native binaries for your host OS).  The builder stage runs `npm ci`
# to install fresh, correct packages inside the Linux container anyway.
node_modules

# --- Build output ------------------------------------------------------------
# dist is generated by `vite build`.  We don't want your local dist (built on
# your machine) to pollute or accidentally override what the builder stage
# produces inside the container.  The builder stage creates its own /app/dist.
dist
dist-ssr

# --- Version control ---------------------------------------------------------
# The .git directory contains your entire commit history.  It has no place
# inside a production image and would bloat the build context significantly.
.git
.gitignore

# --- Environment files -------------------------------------------------------
# NEVER include .env in a Docker image.  Real secrets (API keys, passwords)
# would be baked into an image layer and readable by anyone with image access.
# Pass VITE_* vars as --build-arg at build time (see Dockerfile ARG section)
# and runtime secrets via Docker Compose `environment:` or a secrets manager.
.env
.env.local
.env.*.local

# --- Editor and OS artifacts -------------------------------------------------
# These are purely local tooling artefacts and have zero value inside a
# production container.
.vscode
.idea
.DS_Store
*.suo
*.ntvs*
*.njsproj
*.sln
*.sw?
Thumbs.db

# --- Logs and test reports ---------------------------------------------------
# Logs are runtime output, not source code.  Test results belong in CI
# artefacts, not inside a production image.
logs
*.log
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
pnpm-debug.log*
coverage
playwright-report
test-results

# --- Documentation and non-build files ---------------------------------------
# README, screenshots, and spec files are useful for humans but serve no
# purpose at runtime.  Excluding them keeps the image lean.
README.md
SPEC.txt
screenshots
LICENSE

# --- Docker files themselves -------------------------------------------------
# The Dockerfile and compose file don't need to be inside the image they
# produce.  Excluding them is good hygiene.
Dockerfile
docker-compose.yml
docker-compose*.yml
.dockerignore

# --- CI configuration --------------------------------------------------------
# CI pipeline definitions are for the build server, not the container.
.github
.gitlab-ci.yml
